// Book One, Chapter 7 (session 10). The dark of the moon: the Merchant house asks whether he stays or goes alone.
// Two nights of talk, then the vault, then a word at dawn that leads into chapter 8.
@chapter b1c07
@title The Dark of the Moon
@book burning-city
@version 1

# start
## Two nights
$ do phase("night", 2)
$ b1.coven = "stayed"
$ b1.oath = "full"
$ _heard = 0
Two nights is not long. It is, you find, long enough to learn how many ways a question can be asked.
Prisca has not said another word about it. Nobody has. The house goes about its business with a scrupulous, careful courtesy, and every door that opens for you opens very slowly, so that you can see what is behind it. You are being shown the house. You are being shown, you think, what you would be leaving.
?if b1.prisca_terms == "defied"
  You remember the night you told her no. She kept you anyway, and she has never once reminded you of it, which is a very effective way of reminding you.
?elif b1.prisca_terms == "bargained"
  You remember the night you bargained, and how she gave you three parts of what you asked, and how she watched you take them. She has been waiting ever since to see what a man does who knows his price.
?else
  You remember the terms you took on the first night, and how easily you took them. You were very new. You wonder, now, whether the ease was the point.
?end
-> hub

# hub
## The house
:hub
?if actions_left() == 2
  The first night is warm, and the warehouse is full of the sound of rats and the smell of oil.
?else
  The second night is darker. There is already a thinness in the sky, as if the moon had been rubbed away with a thumb.
?end
